This product includes software developed by DeepSeek:
DeepSeek Harness
https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness, commit fcc0ad4
Licensed under the MIT License (see third_party/deepseek-harness/LICENSE).

third_party/deepseek-harness holds DeepSeek Harness's own files, unmodified:
LICENSE, its goal packages (packages/goal: goal, goal-round-driver, tool-goal
and command-goal, with their tests and documentation) and
docs/subsystems/goal.md. The rest of the harness is left out. These are here
for reference; what runs is the published build of the same version.

Guardian runs DeepSeek Harness's four goal packages from its published npm
packages (@deepseek-ai/dsh-goal, dsh-tool-goal, dsh-goal-round-driver and
dsh-command-goal, with dsh-session, dsh-session-projection, dsh-agent, dsh-llm
and what they need, 0.2.2-rc.1, and @deepseek-ai/cordis 4.1.0, MIT), bundled
into Guardian's hook and MCP programs. They run beside Guardian's own goal
file, which stays the master copy of the goal: they hold a copy of the goal's
phase, revision and round count, their tools can refuse a change, their round
driver is asked about each round, and their prompts are part of what the agent
is sent. Their "complete" is a status change; Guardian's own checks decide
whether a goal is done. Guardian's adapter is packages/core/src/goal/engine.ts.
What is adapted:
- one line of dsh-llm, which reads its own version from a file at run time, is
  inlined at build time (scripts/dsh-bundle.mjs);
- Promise.withResolvers is supplied where the runtime lacks it;
- their runtime gets a stand-in agent, as their own tests use, because the
  agent at work is Cursor's;
- the prompt their round driver hands to its agent is delivered by Cursor's
  stop hook;
- their "armed" state lasts one process and every hook call is a new one, so
  Guardian re-arms the goal with their resume once per stop, and records their
  round-limit block itself when their resume refuses at the cap.

